{:edb/db-before-t 5765 :edb/db-after-t 5766 :edb/tx-data [[13194139539078 50 #inst "2026-10-23T17:52:32.270Z" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186166647 1071 "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{3-2x-x^2} \\textrm{d}x=\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186166647 1071 "$\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{3-2x-x^2} \\textrm{d}x=$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186166651 1071 "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle 2x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3}+y^2\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}=3x^2?\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186166651 1071 "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $2x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3}+y^2\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}=3x^2?$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186166695 1071 "Given that the moment-generating function (MGF) of the random variable $X$ is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle M(t) = \\left( \\dfrac{3}{4} + \\dfrac{1}{4} e^t \\right)^2,\n$$\n\ncompute $\\textrm E[X^k]$, where $k$ is a positive integer." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186166695 1071 "Given that the moment-generating function (MGF) of the random variable $X$ is given by\n$M(t) = \\left( \\dfrac{3}{4} + \\dfrac{1}{4} e^t \\right)^2,$\n\ncompute $\\textrm E[X^k]$, where $k$ is a positive integer." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186166716 1074 "Let $\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i+ Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j$ be a vector field with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf F$ *is conservative if and only if $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\n$$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D = \\mathbb R^2.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ we have $P = e^{y^2} - 2xy$ and $Q = 2xye^{y^2} - y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 2ye^{y^2} - 2x, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 2ye^{y^2}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $\\mathbf F$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf G,$ we have $P = 3x^2 - y^2$ and $Q = 4 - 2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = -2y, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = -2y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2,$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{G}$ is conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf H,$ we have $P = xy^2 + 1$ and $Q = 2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 2xy, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 2y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $\\mathbf{H}$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186166716 1074 "Let $\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i+ Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j$ be a vector field with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D = \\mathbb R^2.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ we have $P = e^{y^2} - 2xy$ and $Q = 2xye^{y^2} - y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 2ye^{y^2} - 2x, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 2ye^{y^2}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $\\mathbf F$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf G,$ we have $P = 3x^2 - y^2$ and $Q = 4 - 2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = -2y, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = -2y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2,$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{G}$ is conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf H,$ we have $P = xy^2 + 1$ and $Q = 2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 2xy, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 2y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $\\mathbf{H}$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186166717 1071 "Consider the vector field $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{2ay}{1+9x^2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\arctan{3x} \\, \\mathbf j\n$$ on $\\mathbb R^2$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186166717 1071 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F = \\dfrac{2ay}{1+9x^2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\arctan{3x} \\, \\mathbf j$ on $\\mathbb R^2$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186166717 1074 "Let $\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i+ Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j$ be a vector field with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf F$ *is conservative if and only if $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\n$$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ the domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ and we have\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{2ay}{1+9x^2}, \\qquad Q = \\arctan{3x}.\n$$\n\n \nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{2a}{1 + 9x^2}, \n\\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{3}{1 + 9x^2}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on $\\mathbb R^2.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2a}{1+9{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{3}{1+9{x}^{2}} \\\\ 2a & =3 \\\\ a & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a = \\dfrac{3}{2}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186166717 1074 "Let $\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i+ Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j$ be a vector field with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ the domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ and we have\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{2ay}{1+9x^2}, \\qquad Q = \\arctan{3x}.\n$$\n\n \nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{2a}{1 + 9x^2}, \n\\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{3}{1 + 9x^2}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on $\\mathbb R^2.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2a}{1+9{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{3}{1+9{x}^{2}} \\\\ 2a & =3 \\\\ a & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a = \\dfrac{3}{2}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186167064 1071 "The third-order differential equation\n\n$y^{‴} + \\cos ty^{″} - t^{2}y^{′} + y = 0$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}_{1}^{\\prime } = {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\\prime } = {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{\\prime } = f ( t )\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nwhere $x_{1}(t) = y(t)$, $x_{2}(t) = y^{′}(t)$, and $x_{3}(t) = y^{″}(t)$. Find the function $f(t)$." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186167064 1071 "The third-order differential equation\n\n$y^{‴} + \\cos ty^{″} - t^{2}y^{′} + y = 0$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}_{1}^{\\prime } = {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\\prime } = {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{\\prime } = f ( t )\\end{array} \\right.$\nwhere $x_{1}(t) = y(t)$, $x_{2}(t) = y^{′}(t)$, and $x_{3}(t) = y^{″}(t)$. Find the function $f(t)$." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186167072 1071 "The third-order initial value problem\n$y^{‴} - y = 0, y(2) = 1, y^{′}(2) =-2, y^{″}(2) =-3$\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{x}_{1}^{\\prime } = {x}_{2} , \\hspace{1em} & {x}_{1} ( 2 ) = p \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\\prime } = {x}_{3} , \\hspace{1em} & {x}_{2} ( 2 ) = q \\\\ {x}_{3}^{\\prime } = f ( t ) , \\hspace{1em} & {x}_{3} ( 2 ) = r\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nwhere $x_{1}(t) = y(t)$, $x_{2}(t) = y^{′}(t)$, $x_{3}(t) = y^{″}(t)$, and $p$, $q$ and $r$ are constants. Calculate $(p - q - r) \\cdot f(t)$." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186167072 1071 "The third-order initial value problem\n$y^{‴} - y = 0, y(2) = 1, y^{′}(2) =-2, y^{″}(2) =-3$\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{x}_{1}^{\\prime } = {x}_{2} , \\hspace{1em} & {x}_{1} ( 2 ) = p \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\\prime } = {x}_{3} , \\hspace{1em} & {x}_{2} ( 2 ) = q \\\\ {x}_{3}^{\\prime } = f ( t ) , \\hspace{1em} & {x}_{3} ( 2 ) = r\\end{array} \\right.$\nwhere $x_{1}(t) = y(t)$, $x_{2}(t) = y^{′}(t)$, $x_{3}(t) = y^{″}(t)$, and $p$, $q$ and $r$ are constants. Calculate $(p - q - r) \\cdot f(t)$." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186167073 1071 "The third-order differential equation $y^{‴} + 7\\sqrt{t}y^{′} + 10y = 0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t)$, where\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle x(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}, A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ a & b & c\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nand for $y = y(t)$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = {x}_{1} ( t ) \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } = {x}_{2} ( t ) \\\\ {y}^{\\prime\\prime } = {x}_{3} ( t ) .\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a + b + c$." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186167073 1071 "The third-order differential equation $y^{‴} + 7\\sqrt{t}y^{′} + 10y = 0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t)$, where\n\n$x(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}, A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ a & b & c\\end{bmatrix}$,\nand for $y = y(t)$, we have\n$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = {x}_{1} ( t ) \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } = {x}_{2} ( t ) \\\\ {y}^{\\prime\\prime } = {x}_{3} ( t ) .\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nFind the value of $a + b + c$." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186167109 1071 "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\frac{e^{-3x^{2}}}{x^{3} + x}\\right)\n$$ at $x = 0.5$. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n> A graphing calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186167109 1071 "Evaluate $\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\frac{e^{-3x^{2}}}{x^{3} + x}\\right)$ at $x = 0.5$. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n> A graphing calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186167123 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)$, defined as\n\n$f(x) = \\frac{\\sin x - x}{x^{2} - x\\cos x}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The function is decreasing on $x ∈ (-2,-1)$\n2. Rounded to three decimal places, $f^{′}(-2) \\approx - 0.255$\n3. Rounded to three decimal places, $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{-1}f(x)dx \\approx 0.217\n$$\n\n> A graphing calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186167123 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)$, defined as\n\n$f(x) = \\frac{\\sin x - x}{x^{2} - x\\cos x}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The function is decreasing on $x ∈ (-2,-1)$\n2. Rounded to three decimal places, $f^{′}(-2) \\approx - 0.255$\n3. Rounded to three decimal places, $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{-1}f(x)dx \\approx 0.217$\n\n> A graphing calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186167124 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)$, defined as\n$f(x) = \\frac{xe^{\\sin x} + 1}{x}$.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The function is increasing on $x ∈ (-4,-2)$\n2. Rounded to three decimal places, $f^{′}(-3) \\approx - 0.971$\n3. Rounded to three decimal places, $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-4}^{-2}f(x)dx \\approx 1.241\n$$\n\n> A graphing calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186167124 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)$, defined as\n$f(x) = \\frac{xe^{\\sin x} + 1}{x}$.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The function is increasing on $x ∈ (-4,-2)$\n2. Rounded to three decimal places, $f^{′}(-3) \\approx - 0.971$\n3. Rounded to three decimal places, $\\displaystyle \\int_{-4}^{-2}f(x)dx \\approx 1.241$\n\n> A graphing calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186167138 1071 "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\operatorname{sech}⁡x(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)\\text{d}x=\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186167138 1071 "$\\int \\operatorname{sech}⁡x(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)\\text{d}x=$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186167139 1071 "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle u(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle v(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nare solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ find $Aw(t)$ where\n$$\n\\displaystyle w(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186167139 1071 "If\n$u(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$v(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}$\nare solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ find $Aw(t)$ where\n$w(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186167158 1074 "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- Systems $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-3{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=\\pi {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- Systems $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyl