2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186167463 1074 "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${u}_{i}$ and ${v}_{i}$ are the $i$th columns of the orthogonal matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively.  \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=2\\sqrt{10}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$ \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186167518 1071 "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{(x-1)\\sqrt{4+2x-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x=\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186167518 1071 "$\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{(x-1)\\sqrt{4+2x-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x=$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186167519 1071 "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{2}{|2x+1|\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4x+5}}\\text{d}x=\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186167519 1071 "$\\int \\frac{2}{|2x+1|\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4x+5}}\\text{d}x=$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186167532 1071 "Given that the moment-generating function of the random variable $X$ is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle M(t) = (\\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{1}{3}e^{t})^{4},\n$$\n\ncompute $E[X]$." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186167532 1071 "Given that the moment-generating function of the random variable $X$ is given by\n$M(t) = (\\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{1}{3}e^{t})^{4}$,\n\ncompute $E[X]$." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186167987 1064 "For a first-order initial value problem, Euler's method approximates the increment in $y$ over a step by using a single slope - the slope at the current point.\n\nThis is simple and works well when the slope stays nearly constant across a step, but can become inaccurate when the slope changes noticeably between successive $x$-values.\n\nThe Runge–Kutta Method improves this idea by sampling the slope at several points within the same step and combining them into a weighted average.\nFor an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the **Runge-Kutta** (**RK4**) **Method** with step size $\\Delta x$ is\n\n$$\n\\Delta y  = \\dfrac16\\left(y' + 2y'_1 + 2y'_2 + y'_3\\right) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- $y'_1 = f(x_1,y_1)$ is the estimated slope at $$\n\\displaystyle (x_1,y_1) = \\left(x + \\dfrac12 \\Delta x,\\: y + \\dfrac12 y' \\cdot \\Delta x\\right)\\!,\n$$\n\n- $y'_2 = f(x_2,y_2)$ is the estimated slope at $$\n\\displaystyle (x_2,y_2) = \\left(x + \\dfrac12 \\Delta x,\\: y + \\dfrac12 y'_1 \\cdot \\Delta x\\right)\\!,\n$$\n\n- $y'_3 = f(x_3, y_3)$ is the estimated slope at $(x_3,y_3) = (x + \\Delta x,\\: y + y'_2 \\cdot \\Delta x).$\n\nInstead of assuming the initial slope represents the entire interval, we take additional “in-between” slope estimates at the midpoint of the step and near its end. This provides a much better estimate of the overall increment in $y$ during the step, while still using only evaluations of $f(x,y).$\n\nWe'll see an example of how to implement the RK4 method soon, but first, let's make sure we know at which points the slope is evaluated in the next example.\n\nImportant note: Many texts denote the four intermediate values by $k_1, k_2, k_3, k_4,$ sometimes scaling each by $\\Delta x,$ but the underlying idea is the same." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186167987 1064 "For a first-order initial value problem, Euler's method approximates the increment in $y$ over a step by using a single slope - the slope at the current point.\n\nThis is simple and works well when the slope stays nearly constant across a step, but can become inaccurate when the slope changes noticeably between successive $x$-values.\n\nThe Runge–Kutta Method improves this idea by sampling the slope at several points within the same step and combining them into a weighted average.\nFor an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the **Runge-Kutta** (**RK4**) **Method** with step size $\\Delta x$ is\n\n$$\n\\Delta y  = \\dfrac16\\left(y' + 2y'_1 + 2y'_2 + y'_3\\right) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- $y'_1 = f(x_1,y_1)$ is the estimated slope at $(x_1,y_1) = \\left(x + \\dfrac12 \\Delta x,\\: y + \\dfrac12 y' \\cdot \\Delta x\\right)\\!,$\n\n- $y'_2 = f(x_2,y_2)$ is the estimated slope at $(x_2,y_2) = \\left(x + \\dfrac12 \\Delta x,\\: y + \\dfrac12 y'_1 \\cdot \\Delta x\\right)\\!,$\n\n- $y'_3 = f(x_3, y_3)$ is the estimated slope at $(x_3,y_3) = (x + \\Delta x,\\: y + y'_2 \\cdot \\Delta x).$\n\nInstead of assuming the initial slope represents the entire interval, we take additional “in-between” slope estimates at the midpoint of the step and near its end. This provides a much better estimate of the overall increment in $y$ during the step, while still using only evaluations of $f(x,y).$\n\nWe'll see an example of how to implement the RK4 method soon, but first, let's make sure we know at which points the slope is evaluated in the next example.\n\nImportant note: Many texts denote the four intermediate values by $k_1, k_2, k_3, k_4,$ sometimes scaling each by $\\Delta x,$ but the underlying idea is the same." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186168016 1064 "Recall that the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′} &=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nhas equilibria at\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\qquad\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h}, 0\\right), \\qquad\n\\left(\\dfrac{c}{d}, \\dfrac{a}{b}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{c}{d}h\\right)\\right).\n$$\n\nNotice that the second component of the third equilibrium point is positive only when \n\n$$\nh < \\dfrac{d}{c},\n$$\n\nwhich guarantees that the third fixed point will lie in the first quadrant. For now, let's consider only this case.\n\nWe now evaluate the Jacobian matrix\n$$\nJ\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}a ( 1 - 2 h x ) - b y & - b x \\\\ d y & - c + d x\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nat the nonzero equilibrium $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 / h \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}$ to get\n\n$$\nJ(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}a ( 1 - 2 h ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{h}} ) ) - b ( 0 ) & - b ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{h}} ) \\\\ d ( 0 ) & - c + d ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{h}} )\\end{array} \\right]}\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}- a & - {\\displaystyle \\frac{b}{h}} \\\\ 0 & {\\displaystyle \\frac{c}{h}} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{d}{c}} - h )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nBecause we are considering the case when $h < \\dfrac{d}{c},$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{c}{h}\\left(\\dfrac{d}{c}-h\\right) > 0.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are $\\lambda_1 = -a < 0$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\lambda_2 = \\dfrac{c}{h}\\left(\\dfrac{d}{c}-h\\right) > 0.\n$$ Since eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is an *unstable saddle*.\n\nAnalyzing the type of the third equilibrium in full generality becomes considerably more cumbersome. Therefore, we'll examine a concrete example later in the lesson. For now, we simply note that the third equilibrium is an *asymptotically stable (spiral) sink*.\n\nA typical phase portrait of the system, in the case when $h < \\dfrac{d}{c},$ looks as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8c82332ed5277586a2b97e900383619a5d61cdb73ff4b60a92de3d5460165165.png)\n\nThe global picture is the following:\n\n- There are two straight-line types of solutions:\n\n- Along the $x$-axis, trajectories approach the equilibrium at $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h},0\\right).$ Indeed, in the absence of the predators, the prey approaches the carrying capacity.\n\n- Along the $y$-axis, trajectories approach the origin. Indeed, in the absence of the prey, the predators decrease exponentially.\n\n- The curved trajectories converge to the equilibrium point $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac{c}{d}, \\dfrac{a}{b}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{c}{d}h\\right)\\right).\n$$\n\nThe corresponding population dynamics graphs look as depicted below.\n\n![](images/a7/a7673170825344e1831f2ba01ce4c20f10091380ff4e116f3b6bd9e7666e51aa.png)" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186168016 1064 "Recall that the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′} &=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nhas equilibria at\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\qquad\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h}, 0\\right), \\qquad\n\\left(\\dfrac{c}{d}, \\dfrac{a}{b}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{c}{d}h\\right)\\right).\n$$\n\nNotice that the second component of the third equilibrium point is positive only when \n\n$$\nh < \\dfrac{d}{c},\n$$\n\nwhich guarantees that the third fixed point will lie in the first quadrant. For now, let's consider only this case.\n\nWe now evaluate the Jacobian matrix\n$$\nJ\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}a ( 1 - 2 h x ) - b y & - b x \\\\ d y & - c + d x\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nat the nonzero equilibrium $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 / h \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}$ to get\n\n$$\nJ(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}a ( 1 - 2 h ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{h}} ) ) - b ( 0 ) & - b ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{h}} ) \\\\ d ( 0 ) & - c + d ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{h}} )\\end{array} \\right]}\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}- a & - {\\displaystyle \\frac{b}{h}} \\\\ 0 & {\\displaystyle \\frac{c}{h}} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{d}{c}} - h )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nBecause we are considering the case when $h < \\dfrac{d}{c},$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{c}{h}\\left(\\dfrac{d}{c}-h\\right) > 0.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are $\\lambda_1 = -a < 0$ and $\\lambda_2 = \\dfrac{c}{h}\\left(\\dfrac{d}{c}-h\\right) > 0.$ Since eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is an *unstable saddle*.\n\nAnalyzing the type of the third equilibrium in full generality becomes considerably more cumbersome. Therefore, we'll examine a concrete example later in the lesson. For now, we simply note that the third equilibrium is an *asymptotically stable (spiral) sink*.\n\nA typical phase portrait of the system, in the case when $h < \\dfrac{d}{c},$ looks as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8c82332ed5277586a2b97e900383619a5d61cdb73ff4b60a92de3d5460165165.png)\n\nThe global picture is the following:\n\n- There are two straight-line types of solutions:\n\n- Along the $x$-axis, trajectories approach the equilibrium at $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h},0\\right).$ Indeed, in the absence of the predators, the prey approaches the carrying capacity.\n\n- Along the $y$-axis, trajectories approach the origin. Indeed, in the absence of the prey, the predators decrease exponentially.\n\n- The curved trajectories converge to the equilibrium point $\\left(\\dfrac{c}{d}, \\dfrac{a}{b}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{c}{d}h\\right)\\right).$\n\nThe corresponding population dynamics graphs look as depicted below.\n\n![](images/a7/a7673170825344e1831f2ba01ce4c20f10091380ff4e116f3b6bd9e7666e51aa.png)" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186168026 1064 "Recall that the equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, for the Lotka–Volterra model \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′} &=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nthe equilibria are the solutions of the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}ax-bxy &=0 \\\\ -cy+dxy &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax-bxy & =0 \\\\ x(a-by) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\dfrac{a}{b}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-cy+d(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\dfrac{a}{b},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-cy+dxy & =0 \\\\ y(-c+dx) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{a}{b}(-c+dx) & =0 \\\\ -c+dx & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{c}{d}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $\\left(\\dfrac{c}{d},\\dfrac{a}{b}\\right).$\n\nTherefore, there are two equilibria:\n\n- The *origin* $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{(0,0)}.$\n\n- The *nonzero equilibrium* $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\left(\\dfrac{c}{d},\\,\\dfrac{a}{b}\\right)}.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186168026 1064 "Recall that the equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, for the Lotka–Volterra model \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′} &=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nthe equili