(nx)\\rangle\n\\!+\\! \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} a_k\\langle \\cos(kx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle\n\\!+\\! \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} b_k\\langle \\sin(kx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle.\n$$\n\n- By orthogonality, $\\langle 1,\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0,$ $\\langle \\sin(kx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0$ for all $k,$ and $\\langle \\cos(kx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0$ whenever $k\\ne n,$ so\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=a_n\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle.\n$$\n\nSince $$\n\\displaystyle \\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} \\cos^2(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\pi,\n$$ we obtain\n\n$$\na_n=\\dfrac{\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle}{\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle}\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\cos(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, we derive the formula for $b_n$ for $n\\ge 1{:}$\n\n- First, we take the inner product of both sides with $\\sin(nx){:}$\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle\n=\n\\left\\langle \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_k\\cos(kx)+b_k\\sin(kx)\\big),\\,\\sin(nx)\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\n- Using linearity, we get\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle\n=\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}\\langle 1,\\sin(nx)\\rangle\n\\!+\\! \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} a_k\\langle \\cos(kx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle\n\\!+\\! \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} b_k\\langle \\sin(kx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle.\n$$\n\n- By orthogonality, $\\langle 1,\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0,$ $\\langle \\cos(kx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0$ for all $k,$ and $\\langle \\sin(kx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0$ whenever $k\\ne n,$ so\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=b_n\\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle.\n$$\n\nSince $$\n\\displaystyle \\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} \\sin^2(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\pi,\n$$ we obtain\n\n$$\nb_n=\\dfrac{\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle}{\\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle}\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\sin(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nPutting these together, the Fourier coefficients are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{