ions of $X_1$ and $X_2$ respectively. Then, we have\n$M_{X_1}(t) = (1-2t)^{-k_1/2}, \\qquad M_{X_2}(t) = (1-2t)^{-k_2/2}$.\nBy the multiplicative property of moment-generating functions, we have that\n$M_{Y}(t)|= M_{X_{1} + X_{2}}(t); = M_{X_{1}}(t) \\cdot M_{X_{2}}(t); = (1 - 2t)^{-k_{1}/2} \\cdot (1 - 2t)^{-k_{2}/2}; = (1 - 2t)^{- (k_{1} + k_{2})/2}$.\nThis is the moment-generating function of a chi-square random variable with distribution $\\chi^2(k_1+k_2)$. Therefore, by the uniqueness property, we conclude\n$Y\\sim \\chi^2(k_1+k_2)$." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186173149 1064 "To evaluate an inverse trigonometric function whose argument is its corresponding function, we need to take extra care.\n\nIt's possible to \"cancel out\" the two functions, but we need to make sure that the input $\\theta$ is in the range of the inverse function. In particular, we have the following results:\n\n- $\\arcsin(\\sin(\\theta)) = \\theta$ provided that $$\n\\displaystyle -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n- $\\arccos(\\cos(\\theta)) = \\theta$ provided that $0\\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi$\n- $\\arctan(\\tan(\\theta)) = \\theta$ provided that $$\n\\displaystyle -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} < \\theta < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose we want to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\arccos\\left(\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\right)\n$$\n\nSince our angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nlies within the range of the $\\arccos$ function, i.e.,\n\n$$\n0\\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\leq \\pi\n$$\n\nwe can write down the answer immediately, giving\n\n$$\n\\arccos\\left(\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\right) = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186173149 1064 "To evaluate an inverse trigonometric function whose argument is its corresponding function, we need to take extra care.\n\nIt's possible to \"cancel out\" the two functions, but we need to make sure that the input $\\theta$ is in the range of the inverse function. In particular, we have the following results:\n\n- $\\arcsin(\\sin(\\theta)) = \\theta$ provided that $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n- $\\arccos(\\cos(\\theta)) = \\theta$ provided that $0\\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi$\n- $\\arctan(\\tan(\\theta)) = \\theta$ provided that $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} < \\theta < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\nFor example, suppose we want to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\arccos\\left(\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\right)\n$$\n\nSince our angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nlies within the range of the $\\arccos$ function, i.e.,\n\n$$\n0\\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\leq \\pi\n$$\n\nwe can write down the answer immediately, giving\n\n$$\n\\arccos\\left(\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\right) = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186173165 1064 "There are three basic so-called *non-degenerate quadratic curves*:\n\n| Standard Equation | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=1\n$$ | Ellipse |\n| $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=1\n$$ | Hyperbola |\n| $x_2^2= 2px_1$ | Parabola |" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186173165 1064 "There are three basic so-called *non-degenerate quadratic curves*:\n\n| Standard Equation | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=1$ | Ellipse |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=1$ | Hyperbola |\n| $x_2^2= 2px_1$ | Parabola |" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186173166 1064 "There are also a few so-called *degenerate (real) quadratic curves:*\n\n| Standard Equation | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0\n$$ | Pair of\n intersecting  lines |\n| ${x_i^2}=a^2$ | Pair of \n parallel lines |\n| ${x_i^2}=0$ | Pair of \n coincident lines |\n| $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0\n$$ | Single point |\n\nLet's see why these equations define the described curves:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0\n$$ is equivalent to $(bx_1 - ax_2)(bx_1 + ax_2)=0$ which in turn gives equations of two lines $bx_1 - ax_2=0$ and $bx_1 + ax_2=0$ intersecting at the origin.\n\n- ${x_i^2}=a^2$ is equivalent to $(x_i - a)(x_i + a)=0$ which in turn gives equations of two parallel lines $x_i =a$ and $x_i=-a.$\n\n- ${x_i^2}=0$ gives equations of two coincident lines $x_i =0$ and $-x_i=0.$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0\n$$ is equivalent to $b^2x_1^2+a^2x_2^2=0$ which in turn gives a single point, the origin, since the sum of two squares is $0$ if and only if both squares are $0.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186173166 1064 "There are also a few so-called *degenerate (real) quadratic curves:*\n\n| Standard Equation | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0$ | Pair of\n intersecting  lines |\n| ${x_i^2}=a^2$ | Pair of \n parallel lines |\n| ${x_i^2}=0$ | Pair of \n coincident lines |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0$ | Single point |\n\nLet's see why these equations define the described curves:\n\n- $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0$ is equivalent to $(bx_1 - ax_2)(bx_1 + ax_2)=0$ which in turn gives equations of two lines $bx_1 - ax_2=0$ and $bx_1 + ax_2=0$ intersecting at the origin.\n\n- ${x_i^2}=a^2$ is equivalent to $(x_i - a)(x_i + a)=0$ which in turn gives equations of two parallel lines $x_i =a$ and $x_i=-a.$\n\n- ${x_i^2}=0$ gives equations of two coincident lines $x_i =0$ and $-x_i=0.$\n\n- $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0$ is equivalent to $b^2x_1^2+a^2x_2^2=0$ which in turn gives a single point, the origin, since the sum of two squares is $0$ if and only if both squares are $0.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186173167 1064 "Finally, some quadratic equations might define an *empty set* (or so-called *imaginary curve*):\n\n| Standard Equation | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=-1\n$$ | Imaginary ellipse |\n| $x_i^2=-a^2$ | Pair of imaginary\n parallel lines |\n\nLet's see why these equations define the described curves:\n\n- Although there are no real solutions, the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2} = -1,\n$$ which is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{x_1^2}{(a\\textrm{i})^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{(b\\textrm{i})^2} = 1,\n$$ has imaginary solutions, so we could interpret this as an imaginary ellipse.\n\n- Although there are no real solutions, the equation ${x_i^2}=-a^2$ is equivalent to $(x_i - a\\textrm{i})(x_i + a\\textrm{i})=0$ which in turn gives equations of two imaginary parallel lines $x_i =a\\textrm{i}$ and $x_i=-a\\textrm{i}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186173167 1064 "Finally, some quadratic equations might define an *empty set* (or so-called *imaginary curve*):\n\n| Standard Equation | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=-1$ | Imaginary ellipse |\n| $x_i^2=-a^2$ | Pair of imaginary\n parallel lines |\n\nLet's see why these equations define the described curves:\n\n- Although there are no real solutions, the equation $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2} = -1,$ which is equivalent to $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{(a\\textrm{i})^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{(b\\textrm{i})^2} = 1,$ has imaginary solutions, so we could interpret this as an imaginary ellipse.\n\n- Although there are no real solutions, the equation ${x_i^2}=-a^2$ is equivalent to $(x_i - a\\textrm{i})(x_i + a\\textrm{i})=0$ which in turn gives equations of two imaginary parallel lines $x_i =a\\textrm{i}$ and $x_i=-a\\textrm{i}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186173169 1064 "The fundamental theorem of line integrals states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a))\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is a piecewise-smooth curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and $f$ is differentiable function such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ \n\nThis theorem tells us that the line integral of a conservative vector field $\\mathbf{F} = \\nabla f$ is determined by the values of $f$ at the endpoints $A$ and $B$ of $C$ only. The integral does *not* depend on the path between $A$ and $B.$ \n\nIn particular, if $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, then we say that $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r\n$$ is **path-independent.**" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186173169 1064 "The fundamental theorem of line integrals states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a))\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is a piecewise-smooth curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and $f$ is differentiable function such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ \n\nThis theorem tells us that the line integral of a conservative vector field $\\mathbf{F} = \\nabla f$ is determined by the values of $f$ at the endpoints $A$ and $B$ of $C$ only. The integral does *not* depend on the path between $A$ and $B.$ \n\nIn particular, if $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, then we say that $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r$ is **path-independent.**" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186173210 1064 "In this lesson, we'll extend our knowledge of absolute value. \n\nLet's first remind ourselves of the rules of absolute value:\n\n- $|{-a}| = |a|$\n\n- $|a \\cdot b| = |a| \\cdot |b|$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\left| \\dfrac{a}{b} \\right| = \\dfrac{|a|}{|b|}\n$$\n\nRecall that the last two rules are known as the **distributive properties.**" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186173210 1064 "In this lesson, we'll extend our knowledge of absolute value. \n\nLet's first remind ourselves of the rules of absolute value:\n\n- $|{-a}| = |a|$\n\n- $|a \\cdot b| = |a| \\cdot |b|$\n\n- $\\left| \\dfrac{a}{b} \\right| = \\dfrac{|a|}{|b|}$\n\nRecall that the last two rules are known as the **distributive properties.**" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186173213 1064 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a sufficiently large population with sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}.$ Let's also assume that the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- $n \\widehat{\\,p} > 5$\n\n- $n (1-\\widehat{\\,p}) > 5$\n\nThen, for a given value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\n\\widehat{\\,p} - z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\\:\n\n\\widehat{\\,p} + z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}\n\\right).\n$$\n\nThe endpoints of our confidence interval are the **confidence limits** and are given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}.\n$$\n\nEach part of the formula above has a name:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion\n\n- $n$ is the sample size\n\n- $z_{\\alpha/2}$ is the corresponding $z$-score\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E = z_{\\alpha/2}\\cdot  \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}\n$$ is the margin of error\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\widehat{\\,p}}_{\\textrm{estimate}} \\pm \\:\\: \\overbrace{ \\underbrace{z_{\\alpha/2}}_{ { {z} \\textrm{-score}}} \\:\\: \\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{red}\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}}_{\\textrm{standard} \\\\ \\:\\:\\:\\textrm{error}} }^{\\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nSo, our confidence interval can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\widehat{\\,p}-[\\text{margin of error}], & \\,\\widehat{\\,p}+[\\text{margin of error}]) \\\\ (\\widehat{\\,p}-[\\text{[math]z[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}], & \\,\\widehat{\\,p}+[\\text{[math]z[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}]) \\\\ (\\widehat{\\,p}-{z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}, & \\,\\widehat{\\,p}+{z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince computing a confidence interval amounts to finding the corresponding confidence limits, we will use the two terms interchangeably.\n\nFinally, in the future, you may assume that if a population is \"large,\" it is sufficiently larger than any sample drawn from it." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186173213 1064 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a sufficiently large population with sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}.$ Let's also assume that the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- $n \\widehat{\\,p} > 5$\n\n- $n (1-\\widehat{\\,p}) > 5$\n\nThen, for a given value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\n\\widehat{\\,p} - z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\\:\n\n\\widehat{\\,p} + z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}\n\\right).\n$$\n\nThe endpoints of our confidence interval are the **confidence limits** and are given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}.\n$$\n\nEach part of the formula above has a name:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion\n\n- $n$ is the sample size\n\n- $z_{\\alpha/2}$ is the corresponding $z$-score\n\n- $E = z_{\\alpha/2}\\cdot  \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}$ is the margin of error\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\widehat{\\,p}}_{\\textrm{estimate}} \\pm \\:\\: \\overbrace{ \\underbrace{z_{\\alpha/2}}_{ { {z} \\textrm{-score}}} \\:\\: \\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{red}\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}}_{\\textrm{standard} \\\\ \\:\\:\\:\\textrm{error}} }^{\\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nSo, our confidence interval can be written as