ight.$.\nIf $F(x, y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$, find $F(\\frac{3}{2}, \\frac{1}{2})$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186176358 1071 "Find the radius of convergence $R$ and interval of convergence $I$ for the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)((x + 1)^{2n})/(n^{2}).\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186176358 1071 "Find the radius of convergence $R$ and interval of convergence $I$ for the series\n\n$∑_(n = 1)^(∞)((x + 1)^{2n})/(n^{2})$." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186176363 1074 "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(x-1)^{2n-1}}{2n-1}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x-1)^{2(n+1)-1}}{2(n+1)-1}\\cdot \\frac{2n-1}{(x-1)^{2n-1}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x-1)^{2n+1}}{2n+1}\\cdot \\frac{2n-1}{(x-1)^{2n-1}}| \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{2}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{2n-1}{2n+1}\\right) \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{2}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{(2-\\frac{1}{n})}{(2+\\frac{1}{n})}\\right) \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{2} \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x-1{|}^{2} & <1 \\\\ |x-1| & <1 \\\\ -1<x-1 & <1 \\\\ 0<x & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the radius of convergence is $R=1,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(0,2).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x-1|=1,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=0$ and $x=2$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=0$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x-1)^{2n-1}}{2n-1} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{((0)-1)^{2n-1}}{2n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{2n-1}}{2n-1} \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{5}-⋯ \\\\  & =-(1+\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{5}+⋯)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the above series in parentheses with $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n},\n$$ we see that the series is divergent by the limit comparison test.\n\n- When $x=2$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x-1)^{2n-1}}{2n-1} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{((2)-1)^{2n-1}}{2n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(1)^{2n-1}}{2n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{5}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the above series with $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n},\n$$ we see that the series is divergent by the limit comparison test.\n\nSo the interval of convergence is $I=(0,2).$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186176363 1074 "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(x-1)^{2n-1}}{2n-1}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x-1)^{2(n+1)-1}}{2(n+1)-1}\\cdot \\frac{2n-1}{(x-1)^{2n-1}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x-1)^{2n+1}}{2n+1}\\cdot \\frac{2n-1}{(x-1)^{2n-1}}| \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{2}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{2n-1}{2n+1}\\right) \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{2}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{(2-\\frac{1}{n})}{(2+\\frac{1}{n})}\\right) \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{2} \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x-1{|}^{2} & <1 \\\\ |x-1| & <1 \\\\ -1<x-1 & <1 \\\\ 0<x & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the radius of convergence is $R=1,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(0,2).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x-1|=1,$ we d