:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{i}!} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{1}!}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{2}!}\\right)⋯\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{n}!}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\sum \\theta }\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-n\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{C}\\,{\\theta }^{S}\\,{e}^{-n\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!\n$$ is a constant and $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=18,\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!=6,\n$$ and $n=16.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{6}\\,{\\theta }^{18}\\,{e}^{-16\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186176372 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=16$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{i}!} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{