 finding the likelihood functions for different distributions and samples." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186179425 1074 "The town's population increases by $1.5\\%$ per year; that is, by a factor of\n\n$$\n(1+0.015)=1.015\n$$\n\nevery year.\n\nLet $t$ denote the number of years and $P(t)$ the population of the town. We can construct a table for the relationship between $t$ and $P(t)$, as follows:\n\n| $t$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $\\ldots$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(t)$ | $15 \\, 000\\cdot(1.015)$ | $15 \\, 000\\cdot(1.015)^2$ | $15 \\, 000\\cdot(1.015)^3$ | $15 \\, 000\\cdot(1.015)^4$ | $\\ldots$ |\n\nTo determine if the relationship is linear or exponential, we need to determine how $P(t)$ changes as $t$ increases at each step:\n\n- For a linear relationship, there should be a common *difference* between successive values of $P(t)$.\n- For an exponential relationship, there should be a common *ratio* between successive values of $P(t)$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. From our table, we see that $P(3) = 15\\,000\\cdot(1.015)^3$.\n- Statement II is false. Let's check for a linear pattern by finding the difference between successive values of $P(t){:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}15000 \\cdot (1.015)^{2} - 15000 \\cdot (1.015) & \\approx 228 \\\\ 15000 \\cdot (1.015)^{3} - 15000 \\cdot (1.015)^{2} & \\approx 232 \\\\ 15000 \\cdot (1.015)^{4} - 15000 \\cdot (1.015)^{3} & \\approx 235\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nThis list of differences does not show a definite common difference.\n- Statement III is true. Let's check for a common ratio by dividing successive values of $P(t){:}$\n$(15000 \\cdot (1.015)^{2})/(15000 \\cdot (1.015))|= 1.015; (15000 \\cdot (1.015)^{3})/(15000 \\cdot (1.015)^{2})|= 1.015; (15000 \\cdot (1.015)^{4})/(15000 \\cdot (1.015)^{3})|= 1.015$\nThis list of quotients shows a definite common ratio of $1.015$.\n\nTherefore, the function that best models the relationship 