ad, let's recall the following special limit:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin x}{x} =1\n$$We rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{θ}|= lim_(θ → 0)(2 \\cdot \\frac{\\sin 2θ}{2θ}); = 2 \\cdot lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{\\sin2θ}{2θ}\n$$Let $x = 2\\theta$. Then, since $x\\to0$ as $\\theta \\to 0$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle 2 \\cdot \\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin \\color{blue}2\\theta}{\\color{blue}2\\theta} = 2 \\cdot \\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin \\color{blue}x}{\\color{blue}x} = 2 \\cdot 1 =2.\n$$\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0} \\: \\dfrac{1}{\\cos 2\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos 0} = \\dfrac11 = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nlim_(θ → 0)\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{θ} \\cdot lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{1}{\\cos 2θ} = 2 \\cdot 1 = 2\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186179609 1074 "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(θ → 0)\\frac{\\tan 2θ}{θ} &= lim_(θ → 0)(\\left(\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{\\cos 2θ}\\right))/(θ) \\\\\n&= lim_(θ → 0)(\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{θ} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cos 2θ}) \\\\\n&= lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{θ} \\cdot lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{1}{\\cos 2θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0$, both numerator and denominator approach $0$. So, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n$\\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin 2\\theta}{\\theta} = \\dfrac{0}{0}$,\nwhich is an indeterminate form. Instead, let's recall the following special limit:\n$\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin x}{x} =1$We rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n$lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{θ}|= lim_(θ → 0)(2 \\cdot \\frac{\\sin 2θ}{2θ}); = 2 \\cdot lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{\\sin2θ}{2θ}$Let $x = 2\\theta$. Then, since $x\\to0$ as $\\theta \\to 0$, we have\n$2 \\cdot \\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin \\color{blue}2\\theta}{\\color{blue}2\\theta} = 2 \\cdot \\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin \\color{blue}x}{\\color{blue}x} = 2 \\cdot 1 =2$.\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n$\\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0} \\: \\dfrac{1}{\\cos 2\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos 0} = \\dfrac11 = 1$.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nlim_(θ → 0)\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{θ} \\cdot lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{1}{\\cos 2θ} = 2 \\cdot 1 = 2\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186179610 1074 "Let's denote the generating function of our sequence as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n\n$$\n\nWe start by multiplying both sides of the recurrence relation by $x^n$. This gives\n\n$$\na_n x^n + 4a_{n-1}x^n = (-2)^n x^n\n$$\n\nIf we sum this over all values of\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_n x^n + \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty 4a_{n-1}x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty (-2)^n x^n\n$$\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_n x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n - a_0 = f(x) - 1\n$$\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty 4a_{n-1}x^n = 4x \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n-1}x^{n-1} = 4x \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n = 4x f(x)\n$$\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty (-2)^n x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty (-2x)^n = \\dfrac{-2x}{1-(-2x)} = -\\dfrac{2x}{1+2x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f(x) - 1) + 4xf(x) &= -\\frac{2x}{1 + 2x} \\\\\nf(x)(1 + 4x) - 1 &= -\\frac{2x}{1 + 2x} \\\\\nf(x)(1 + 4x) &= 1 - \\frac{2x}{1 + 2x} \\\\\nf(x)(1 + 4x) &= \\frac{1}{1 + 2x} \\\\\nf(x) &= (1)/((1 + 2x)(1 + 4x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186179610 1074 "Let's denote the generating function of our sequence as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n\n$$\n\nWe start by multiplying both sides of the recurrence relation by $x^n$. This gives\n\n$$\na_n x^n + 4a_{n-1}x^n = (-2)^n x^n\n$$\n\nIf we sum this over all values of\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_n x^n + \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty 4a_{n-1}x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty (-2)^n x^n\n$$\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_n x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n - a_0 = f(x) - 1$\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty 4a_{n-1}x^n = 4x \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n-1}x^{n-1} = 4x \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n = 4x f(x)$\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty (-2)^n x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty (-2x)^n = \\dfrac{-2x}{1-(-2x)} = -\\dfrac{2x}{1+2x}$\n\nTherefore, our equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f(x) - 1) + 4xf(x) &= -\\frac{2x}{1 + 2x} \\\\\nf(x)(1 + 4x) - 1 &= -\\frac{2x}{1 + 2x} \\\\\nf(x)(1 + 4x) &= 1 - \\frac{2x}{1 + 2x} \\\\\nf(x)(1 + 4x) &= \\frac{1}{1 + 2x} \\\\\nf(x) &= (1)/((1 + 2x)(1 + 4x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186179611 1071 "Find the generating function for the sequence that satisfies the recurrence relation $a_n-a_{n-1}=1-3n$ with $a_0=2$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty n x^n = \\dfrac{x}{(1-x)^2}.\n$$*" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186179611 1071 "Find the generating function for the sequence that satisfies the recurrence relation $a_n-a_{n-1}=1-3n$ with $a_0=2$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty n x^n = \\dfrac{x}{(1-x)^2}$.*" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186179611 1074 "Let's denote the generating function of our sequence as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n\n$$\n\nWe start by multiplying both sides of the recurrence relation by $x^n$. This gives\n\n$$\na_nx^n -a_{n-1}x^n =(1-3n) x^n\n$$\n\nIf we sum this over all values of\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_n x^n - \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n-1}x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty (1-3n) x^n\n$$\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_n x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n - a_0 = f(x) - 2\n$$\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n-1}x^n = x \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n-1}x^{n-1} = x \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_{n}x^n = x f(x)\n$$\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty (1-3n) x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty x^n -3 \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty n x^n = \\dfrac{x}{1-x}-\\dfrac{3x}{(1-x)^2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f(x) - 2) - xf(x) &= \\frac{x}{1 - x} - (3x)/((1 - x)^{2}) \\\\\nf(x)(1 - x) - 2 &= (-2x - x^{2})/((1 - x)^{2}) \\\\\nf(x)(1 - x) &= (-2x - x^{2})/((1 - x)^{2}) + 2 \\\\\nf(x)(1 - x) &= (x^{2} - 6x + 2)/((1 - x)^{2}) \\\\\nf(x) &= (x^{2} - 6x + 2)/((1 - x)^{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186179611 1074 "Let's denote the generating function of our sequence as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n\n$$\n\nWe start by multiplying both sides of the recurrence relation by $x^n$. This gives\n\n$$\na_nx^n -a_{n-1}x^n =(1-3n) x^n\n$$\n\nIf we sum this over all values of\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_n x^n - \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n-1}x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty (1-3n) x^n\n$$\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_n x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n - a_0 = f(x) - 2$\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n-1}x^n = x \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n-1}x^{n-1} = x \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_{n}x^n = x f(x)$\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty (1-3n) x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty x^n -3 \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty n x^n = \\dfrac{x}{1-x}-\\dfrac{3x}{(1-x)^2}$\n\nTherefore, our equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f(x) - 2) - xf(x) &= \\frac{x}{1 - x} - (3x)/((1 - x)^{2}) \\\\\nf(x)(1 - x) - 2 &= (-2x - x^{2})/((1 - x)^{2}) \\\\\nf(x)(1 - x) &= (-2x - x^{2})/((1 - x)^{2}) + 2 \\\\\nf(x)(1 - x) &= (x^{2} - 6x + 2)/((1 - x)^{2}) \\\\\nf(x) &= (x^{2} - 6x + 2)/((1 - x)^{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186179612 1074 "Let's denote the generating function of our sequence as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n\n$$\n\nWe start by multiplying both sides of the recurrence relation by $x^n$. This gives\n\n$$\na_n x^n + a_{n-1}x^n + a_{n-2} x^n = x^n\n$$\n\nIf we sum this over all values of\n\n$$\nn \\geq 2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty a_n x^n + \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty a_{n-1}x^n + \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty a_{n-2}x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty x^n. \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThe idea now is to write each of the summations in equation $(\\ast)$ terms of $f(x)$.\n\n- The first sum on the left-hand side is almost the same as $f(x)$. It's just missing the first two terms. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty a_n x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n - a_0 - a_1 x = f(x) - 1\n$$\n\n- The second sum on the left-hand side has a mismatch between the indexes. To correct this, we can pull a single factor of $x$ outside the sum, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty a_{n-1}x^n = x \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty a_{n-1}x^{n-1} = x \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n}x^{n}\n$$\nThen, we recognize that the summation is almost $f(x)$, but the first term is missing. Therefore, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle x \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n}x^{n} = x \\left(\\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_{n}x^n - a_0 \\right) = x (f(x) - 1).\n$$\n- For the third term, we can pull out a factor of $x^2$ to correct the mismatch of the indexes, which gives\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty a_{n-2}x^n = x^2 \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty a_{n-2}x^{n-2} = x^2 \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_{n}x^n = x^2 f(x)\n$$\n\n- Finally, the right-hand side can be evaluated as a geometric series:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty x^n = \\dfrac{x^2}{1-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation $(\\ast)$ can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f(x) - 1) + x(f(x) - 1) + x^{2}f(x) &= \\frac{x^{2}}{1 - x} \\\\\nf(x)(1 + x + x^{2}) - 1 - x &= \\frac{x^{2}}{1 - x} \\\\\nf(x)(1 + x + x^{2}) &= \\frac{x^{2}}{1 - x} + 1 + x \\\\\nf(x)(1 + x + x^{2}) &= \\frac{1}{1 - x} \\\\\nf(x) &= (1)/((1 - x)(1 + x + x^{2}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186179612 1074 "Let's denote the generating function of our sequence as\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n\n$$\n\nWe start by multiplying both sides of the recurrence relation by $x^n$. This gives\n\n$$\na_n x^n + a_{n-1}x^n + a_{n-2} x^n = x^n\n$$\n\nIf we sum this over all values of\n\n$$\nn \\geq 2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty a_n x^n + \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty a_{n-1}x^n + \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty a_{n-2}x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty x^n. \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThe idea now is to write each of the summations in equation $(\\ast)$ terms of $f(x)$.\n\n- The first sum on the left-hand side is almost the same as $f(x)$. It's just missing the first two terms. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty a_n x^n = \\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n - a_0 - a_1 x = f(x) - 1\n$$\n\n- The second sum on the left-hand side has a mismatch between the indexes. To correct this, we can pull a single factor of $x$ outside the sum, as follows:\n$\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty a_{n-1}x^n = x \\sum\\limits_{n=2}^\\infty a_{n-1}x^{n-1} = x \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n}x^{n}$\nThen, we recognize that the summation is almost $f(x)$, but the first term is missing. Therefore, we have\n$x \\sum\\limits_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n}x^{n} = x \\left(\\sum\\limits_{n=0}^\\infty a_{n}x^n - a_0